    "description": "A Fleet Telemetry service receives pressure readings from pressure sensors, temperature readings from refrigeration sensors, and cargo weights from scales. Each sensor supplies a numeric reading plus a unit code, while the Alerts component compares readings against threshold bands with lower and upper bounds and the Reporting component aggregates readings by hour. Readings can arrive in pascals, bar, or kilopascals; weights can arrive in kilograms, pounds, or tonnes. The Aggregation component stores readings in pascals and kilograms, but the Audit Export component must reproduce the original value and unit. The Threshold component must reject combinations whose dimensions do not match, and the API must accept input such as \"2500 kg\" without every endpoint implementing its own parser. Bare numeric fields are causing duplicate conversion factors, accidental addition of incompatible measurements, and inconsistent export formatting. The model must keep amount and unit together through ingestion, calculation, persistence, threshold evaluation, and display, while still allowing canonical-unit conversion at clear boundaries.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the domain representation that keeps each measurement's amount and unit inseparable across ingestion, conversion, comparison, arithmetic, storage, and formatting.",
    "notes": "quantity primary: amount and unit stay inseparable across ingestion, conversion, comparison, storage, and formatting; range is the threshold-band near-miss (description now states bands with bounds)."
